  "raw_label": "1",
  "texts": {
    "preamble": "Revision vs Susheela on 14 January, 2016 Author: B.Sudheendra Kumar Bench: B.Sudheendra Kumar In The High Court Of Kerala At Ernakulam Present: The Honourable Mr. Justice B.Sudheendra Kumar Thursday, The 14Th Day Of January 2016/24Th Pousha, 1937 Crl.Rev.Pet.No. 2168 of 2009 ( ) --------------------------------- Crl.A.108/2007 Of The Additional Sessions Court, Kottayam Cc 989/2001 Of The Judicial First Class Magistrate Court, Changanacherry Revision Petitioners/Appellant No.4,5, & 6/Accused Nos.7,9 & 11 --------------------------------------------------------------- 1. Susheela, D/O. Krishnankutty, Chirackal House, Perumbaikkad, Kottayam. 2. Shiny, D/O. P.K.Bhaskaran, Plamthottathil House, Thottakkattu, Koyippuram Thiruvalla. 3. Sheena S.Babu, D/O. Sadasivan, Shine Nivas, Malloosserry, Perumbaikkadu Kottayam. By Advs.Sri.M.G.Karthikeyan Sri.Nireesh Mathew Respondents/Respondents/Complainant & State: ---------------------------------------------- 1. The Food Inspector, Changanacherry Circle, Changanacherry Kottayam District, Rep.By Public Prosecutor High Court Of Kerala, Ernakulam. 2. State Of Kerala, Represented By Revision vs Susheela on 14 January, 2016 Indian Kanoon - http://indiankanoon.org/doc/82778777/ 1 The Public Prosecutor, High Court Of Kerala Ernakulam.",
    "facts": "2. Heard. 3. The prosecution allegation can be briefly stated thus:- On 7.2.2001 at 3.30 p.m., PW1, the Food Inspector purchased 750 ml. of toddy from the shop of the first accused. Accused Nos.2 to 12 were the licencee of the said shop. After sampling as per rules, one sample was sent for analysis to the Public Analyst. After analysis, the Public Analyst submitted Ext. P12 report stating that the sample did not conform to the standard prescribed for toddy under the Prevention of Food Adulteration Rules and was therefore adulterated. Thereafter, on the request of the accused, second sample was sent to the Central Food Laboratory for analysis. As per Ext.P36 certificate of the Central Food Laboratory, the sample did not conform to the standard prescribed for toddy as per the Prevention of Food Adulteration Rules and was therefore adulterated. 4. Before the trial Court, PW1 to PW5 were examined and Exts. P1 to P36 were marked for the prosecution. DW1 was examined for the revision petitioner.",
    "arguments": "5. The learned counsel for the revision petitioner has submitted that since no laboratory for the analysis of the food item was defined and no validated method of analysis was prescribed as per the Revision vs Susheela on 14 January, 2016 Indian Kanoon - http://indiankanoon.org/doc/82778777/ 2 mandate of Section 23 (1-A)(ee) and (hh) of the PFA Act, Ext.P36 cannot be acted upon to hold that the food item purchased by the Food Inspector was adulterated. The learned counsel for the revision petitioner relied on the decision of the Apex Court in Pepsico India Holdings (P) Ltd. v. Food Inspector [(2011) 1 SCC 176] to buttress his argument. 6. In this case, admittedly, the laboratory for the analysis of food item was not defined and no validated method of analysis of the food item was prescribed as mandated under Section 23 (1-A) (ee) and (hh) of the P.F.A. Act. Therefore, Ext.P36 certificate of analysis cannot be acted upon to hold that the food item purchased in this case was adulterated in view of the decision of the Apex court in Pepsico India Holdings (supra). It was held by the Supreme Court in Pepsico India Holdings (supra) that since the Laboratories were not notified and the tests conducted by such laboratories were not admissible in evidence, no prosecution could be based on such report and the accused was entitled to get acquittal. In the decision of this Court in Gopalakrishnan v. Food Inspector [2013 (3) KLT 455], the court relied on the decision of the Supreme Court in Pepsico (supra) and found that the Public Analyst's report was not admissible in evidence and accordingly, the Court acquitted the accused. The matter was referred to a Division Bench and the Division Bench also affirmed the said decision, submitted by the learned counsel for the petitioner and the learned Public Prosecutor.",
    "petitioner_arguments": "5. The learned counsel for the revision petitioner has submitted that since no laboratory for the analysis of the food item was defined and no validated method of analysis was prescribed as per the Revision vs Susheela on 14 January, 2016 Indian Kanoon - http://indiankanoon.org/doc/82778777/ 2 mandate of Section 23 (1-A)(ee) and (hh) of the PFA Act, Ext.P36 cannot be acted upon to hold that the food item purchased by the Food Inspector was adulterated. The learned counsel for the revision petitioner relied on the decision of the Apex Court in Pepsico India Holdings (P) Ltd. v. Food Inspector [(2011) 1 SCC 176] to buttress his argument. 6. In this case, admittedly, the laboratory for the analysis of food item was not defined and no validated method of analysis of the food item was prescribed as mandated under Section 23 (1-A) (ee) and (hh) of the P.F.A. Act. Therefore, Ext.P36 certificate of analysis cannot be acted upon to hold that the food item purchased in this case was adulterated in view of the decision of the Apex court in Pepsico India Holdings (supra).",
    "respondent_arguments": "",
    "other_lawyer_arguments": "It was held by the Supreme Court in Pepsico India Holdings (supra) that since the Laboratories were not notified and the tests conducted by such laboratories were not admissible in evidence, no prosecution could be based on such report and the accused was entitled to get acquittal. In the decision of this Court in Gopalakrishnan v. Food Inspector [2013 (3) KLT 455], the court relied on the decision of the Supreme Court in Pepsico (supra) and found that the Public Analyst's report was not admissible in evidence and accordingly, the Court acquitted the accused. The matter was referred to a Division Bench and the Division Bench also affirmed the said decision, submitted by the learned counsel for the petitioner and the learned Public Prosecutor."
  },
